.\" zdbviewall(1) — comprehensive all-in-one man page for zdbview
.\" Modeled after zshall(1): every section of the reference in one document.
.\" Render with:  man -l man/man1/zdbviewall.1
.\" Install:      cp man/man1/zdbviewall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH ZDBVIEWALL 1 "2026-08-29" "zdbview 0.13.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zdbviewall \- the zdbview comprehensive reference (detection, SQLite CRUD, rkyv
inspection, keys, search, recent files, storage format, architecture)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all-in-one
.B zdbview
manual, modeled on
.BR zshall (1).
It concatenates the major sections of the reference into a single page so it can
be grepped with `/':
.RS 2
.PP
.B 1.  INVOCATION
.RB "\(em " "arguments, flags, what happens with no file"
.br
.B 2.  FILE TYPE DETECTION
.RB "\(em " "header magic, why the extension is not trusted, fallbacks"
.br
.B 3.  SQLITE MODE
.RB "\(em " "table list, paging, cell edit, insert, delete, raw SQL"
.br
.B 4.  RKYV MODE
.RB "\(em " "the format registry, Records/Info/Strings/Hex views"
.br
.B 5.  KEYS
.RB "\(em " "every binding in every mode, including modal input"
.br
.B 6.  SEARCH
.RB "\(em " "what is scanned per mode, wrap-around, n/N"
.br
.B 7.  RECENT FILES
.RB "\(em " "the picker, on-disk format, atomic writes, cap"
.br
.B 8.  ARCHITECTURE
.RB "\(em " "module map and where each behavior lives"
.br
.B 9.  EXAMPLES
.RB "\(em " "the common invocations"
.br
.B 10. LIMITATIONS
.RB "\(em " "what is deliberately not implemented"
.br
.B 11. EXPORT, DETAIL AND CLIPBOARD
.RB "\(em " "CSV/JSON out, the detail screen, OSC 52 copy"
.RE
.SH 1. INVOCATION
.RS 4
.B zdbview
.RI [ \-\-sqlite | \-\-rkyv ]
.RI [ FILE ]
.RE
.PP
With a
.IR FILE ,
zdbview detects the store kind and opens it. With no
.IR FILE ,
it shows the recent-files picker; quitting the picker exits with status 0
without opening anything.
.TP
.B \-\-sqlite
Skip detection and open the file as a SQLite database. If SQLite refuses the
file, zdbview falls back to the binary inspector rather than failing.
.TP
.B \-\-rkyv
Skip detection and open the file as a rkyv archive / raw binary. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-sqlite ;
clap rejects the combination.
.TP
.BI \-\-export " FORMAT"
Dump the file to standard output and exit, without initializing the terminal.
.I FORMAT
is
.B json
or
.BR csv ,
and a
.I FILE
is required. See section 11.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH 2. FILE TYPE DETECTION
Detection reads the first 16 bytes and compares them to the SQLite header magic
.RB ( "SQLite format 3\e0" ).
.TP
.B Full header readable
The magic decides, alone. Present \(rA SQLite. Absent \(rA binary inspector. The
file name is not consulted.
.TP
.B File shorter than 16 bytes
No header can exist, so the extension is used as a tie-breaker:
.BR .db ,
.BR .sqlite ,
.B .sqlite3
open as SQLite; anything else opens in the inspector.
.TP
.B Explicit flag
.B \-\-sqlite
or
.B \-\-rkyv
short-circuits both rules.
.PP
Trusting the magic over the extension is deliberate. rkyv cache shards are
routinely written under a
.B .db
name, and an extension-first reader would hand those to SQLite, which reports a
corrupt-database error for what is a perfectly valid archive.
.SH 3. SQLITE MODE
.SS Layout
The left pane lists every
.B table
and
.B view
in
.BR sqlite_master ,
excluding internal
.BR sqlite_% ,
sorted by name. The right pane shows one page of rows for the selection. The
status line at the bottom carries the key hints and the result of the last
operation.
.SS Paging
Pages hold 500 rows.
.B Ctrl\-f
/
.B PageDown
advance one page,
.B Ctrl\-b
/
.B PageUp
go back, and the pane title shows
.RI "\(oq" "rows <from>..<to> of <total>" "\(cq" .
.B G
walks to the last page and selects its last row;
.B gg
returns to the first row of the first page.
.SS Row addressing
Every edit and delete targets a row by its
.BR rowid ,
selected alongside the real columns as
.BR "SELECT rowid, * FROM <table>" .
That is what makes the CRUD generic: no schema knowledge, no primary-key
guessing, no per-database configuration. A
.B WITHOUT ROWID
table exposes no such handle, so its rows are listed but edit and delete are
refused with a status-line message.
.SS Editing a cell
.B e
opens a modal seeded with the current display value. Enter runs
.PP
.RS 4
.B UPDATE "<table>" SET "<column>" = ?1 WHERE rowid = ?2
.RE
.PP
with the typed text bound as a parameter (so quoting never breaks it), then
reloads the page. Esc cancels. Identifiers are double-quoted and internal
quotes doubled, so table and column names containing spaces or keywords work.
.SS Inserting a row
.B a
runs
.B "INSERT INTO <table> DEFAULT VALUES" .
Columns take their declared defaults, or NULL. Tables with a
.B NOT NULL
column and no default reject this, and the SQLite error is shown in the status
line \(em use
.B :
to insert explicit values instead.
.SS Deleting a row
.B d
asks for confirmation;
.B y
runs
.B "DELETE FROM <table> WHERE rowid = ?1"
and any other key cancels. This is the only destructive operation that
confirms; a cell edit is committed directly.
.SS Raw SQL
.B :
opens a command line for one arbitrary statement. The affected-row count is
reported and the current page reloaded, so
.BR ALTER ,
.BR CREATE ,
.BR DROP ,
bulk
.B UPDATE
and
.B DELETE
all work. Values are displayed by type: integers and reals verbatim, text as
UTF-8 (lossy), NULL as
.BR NULL ,
and blobs as
.RI "\(oq" "<blob N bytes>" "\(cq" .
.SH 4. RKYV MODE
.SS Why a registry is needed at all
An rkyv archive is a memory image of a Rust value plus a root pointer. The
format stores no field names, no type tags and no length-prefixed schema; the
originating Rust type
.I is
the schema. A reader that does not have that type cannot know whether eight
bytes are a u64, two u32s, or a relative pointer \(em so a
.I generic
field-name editor is impossible, and zdbview does not pretend otherwise. What it
does instead is carry the type for the formats it knows.
.SS The format registry
.I src/formats.rs
holds a byte-compatible copy of each recognized producer's archive type: same
rkyv version (0.7), same features
.RB ( validation ", " archive_le ", " size_32 ),
same field order and types. Detection is a cheap little-endian scan for the
format's magic, followed by a full
.B rkyv::check_archived_root
validation. Every format below is recognized \(em the script and heap caches the
fusevm-hosted languages write:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
.B ZRSC
\(em the zshrs script cache: per-path entries carrying mtime, cache timestamps
and the compiled chunk blob.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ZRAL
\(em the zshrs autoload cache: per-function entries carrying cache timestamps
and the compiled chunk blob.
.IP \(bu 2
.B STRY
\(em the strykelang script cache, in both its native and its compat layout;
keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B AWKR
\(em the awkrs script cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B VIML
\(em the vimlrs script cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B TEXR
\(em the texrs bytecode cache: the compiled chunk for a `.tex` document, keyed
by document path.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ELSP
\(em the elisprs heap-image cache, keyed by script path.
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B pythonrs
bytecode cache \(em no magic header, keyed by source path.
.IP \(bu 2
the hash-keyed
.B rubylang
/
.B arb
script cache \(em no magic header, keyed by a u64 content hash rendered as hex.
.RE
.PP
.SS Registering a magic
The tag space belongs to producers, not to this viewer: the header those tags
stamp is specified on its own terms in
.I spec/rkyv\-shard\-header.md
and a tag is claimed by publishing it, with no authority to apply to. So the
built-in list above is not the closed set.
.I src/magics.rs
merges
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zdbview/magics
over it \(em one
.RI "\(oq" "tag = display name" "\(cq"
per line, the tag being four printable ASCII characters or an explicit
.B 0x
hex u32 \(em so a producer zdbview has never heard of is detected by the startup
scan and named in the UI without a patch or a release. A registered tag with no
copied archive type is still worth having: the file is offered under its real
name, and the Info view names the producer over
.RB \(oq "registered magic, no decoder in this build" \(cq
instead of calling the archive unknown.
An entry for a tag zdbview ships renames it; the decoder keyed on that tag is
unaffected.
.B \-\-formats
prints the resolved registry, marking what came from the file.
.PP
The two header-less formats have nothing to pre-filter on, so they are attempted
last and admitted on rkyv validation alone. An unrelated archive that happens to
survive the earlier magic scans still fails their validation and falls through to
the structural view.
.PP
Because decoding goes through validation, a copied type that has drifted from
its producer fails the check and the file falls back to the structural views. A
mismatch is therefore visible as "unrecognized", never as silently wrong fields.
Adding a format is one registry entry: copy the type, map the magic.
.SS Records (key 0)
The key/value table for a recognized archive: keys on the left, the selected
value's decoded scalar fields plus a hex dump of its blob on the right. Selected
automatically whenever a format is recognized;
.B /
searches the keys. The view is read-only \(em writing an archive back means
reproducing the producer's exact layout, and a wrong write corrupts a cache the
producer then mmaps.
.SS Info (key 1)
Path, byte size, and the number of extracted string runs; for a recognized
archive, also the detected format name and its decoded header fields
(format version, producing zshrs version, pointer width, build timestamp).
.SS Strings (key 2)
Every run of at least 4 consecutive printable ASCII bytes
.RB ( 0x20 \(en 0x7e ),
listed as
.RI "\(oq" "offset  text" "\(cq"
with the offset in hex. For rkyv archives written by the MenkeTechnologies
stack this is the useful view: it surfaces command names, completion keys,
interned identifiers and every string field, in archive order.
.SS Hex (key 3)
An
.BR xxd (1)-style
dump: 16 bytes per line as
.RI "\(oq" "offset  hex bytes  |ascii|" "\(cq" ,
with a gap after the eighth byte and non-printable bytes shown as
.BR . .
.B j
/
.B k
scroll a line,
.B PageDown
/
.B PageUp
scroll 16 lines, and
.B G
jumps to the final line.
.SH 5. KEYS
.SS Global
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Leave the current screen; from the main view, quit.
.TP
.B ?
Show the context-sensitive key-help overlay. Any key closes it.
.TP
.B y
Copy the current cell, value or record key to the system clipboard via the
OSC 52 terminal escape (em no clipboard library, and it works over SSH.
.TP
.B x
Export: the current table as CSV (SQLite) or all decoded records as JSON (rkyv),
written to a file in the working directory.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
First / last. A lone
.B g
arms the motion; any other key disarms it.
.TP
.B /
Start a search.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Next / previous match.
.SS SQLite mode
.TP
.B Tab
Toggle focus between the table list and the row grid.
.TP
.B Enter
From the table list, move focus to the rows; from the row grid, open the detail
screen for the selected row.
.TP
.BR H ", " U
Hide the column under the cursor, or show every hidden column again. The last
visible column cannot be hidden. The cursor steps over hidden columns rather
than selecting one invisibly.
.TP
.B f
Freeze the columns up to and including the cursor at the left edge; pressing it
inside the frozen span unfreezes them. A wide table scrolls sideways under the
cursor while the frozen columns stay put.
.TP
.B #
Show the rowid as a leading read-only column.
.TP
.BR m ", " M
Step the cursor column's display format forwards or back through DB Browser's
eighteen: decimal, exponent, hex blob, hex number, octal, round, lower and upper
case, dd/mm/yyyy, julian day, unix epoch in UTC and local time, Apple NSDate,
Java milliseconds, WebKit microseconds in UTC and local time, and the Windows
OLE date. A format is a SQL expression applied in the SELECT, not a rendering
rule — which is the only way to reach a blob's bytes, since the grid receives
cells that are already display strings. The column keeps its own name, so
sorting, filtering and editing still address the raw value.
.TP
.B !
Manage the conditional formats for the column under the cursor: rules that paint
a cell when its value matches.
.B a
adds a rule,
.B Enter
edits its condition,
.B c
cycles the colour,
.B b
toggles bold,
.B d
drops it, and
.BR J / K
reorder — the first rule that matches wins. A condition is an operator and a
value (> 5, = done, <> 0, like a%), null / not null, or bare text meaning
\(lqcontains\(rq. Comparisons are numeric when both sides read as numbers and
textual otherwise.
.TP
.B r
Find and replace in the column under the cursor, asked in two steps: what to
find, then what to put there. Between them it counts the rows that match, so a
term that finds nothing never reaches the second question. The replacement is a
substring replace over the rows the active filter leaves, and it lands in the
edit buffer like any other write.
.TP
.B i
Insert a row value by value. A column starts NULL and stays NULL unless
something is typed, so NULL and the empty string stay apart;
.B n
puts a column back to NULL.
.TP
.B V
Save the current filter as a view. The filter's patterns are written into the
statement as literals, since a view cannot carry parameters.
.TP
.BR z ", " Z
Clear the sorting, or the filter.
.TP
.B L
Unlock a view for editing. A view can only be written to through INSTEAD OF
triggers, so this reports whether the view has them; editing one without them is
refused with that reason.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-y
Copy the column's name to the clipboard.
.TP
.B :
Open the SQL editor. Inside it, the Alt chords are DB Browser's Execute SQL
menu:
.BR Alt\-t / Alt\-w
open and close a tab,
.BR Alt\-[ / Alt\-]
move between them,
.BR Alt\-o / Alt\-s
load and save a .sql file,
.B Alt\-l
runs only the line the cursor is on,
.B Alt\-;
comments it,
.BR Alt\-f / Alt\-r
find and replace inside the statement,
.B Alt\-x
exports the last result set as CSV or JSON by extension,
.B Alt\-v
saves the statement that produced it as a view, and
.B Alt\-W
wraps the transcript. Each tab keeps its own statement, transcript and file.
Esc stops a running statement: the statement runs on the drawing thread, so the
key is read from SQLite's progress handler, which is the only place that gets
control back while it runs.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-n
Put NULL in the cell — not the empty string, which is what clearing it would
write.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-h
Copy the cell as a hex and ASCII dump.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-e
Write the cell's bytes to a temporary file and open it with the desktop's own
opener (open, or xdg-open).
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-r ", " F5
Re-read the database, dropping every cached fact including the reader threads'.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-p
Print the table, under its filter and sort, by piping it to lpr — which is the
print dialog a terminal program has.
.TP
.B P
Open the editable pragmas: the eighteen settings DB Browser puts in its Edit
Pragmas tab, which decide how the database is written rather than what is in it.
Space cycles a flag or a named set, Enter types a number, Esc goes back. Every
change is read back from the database and reported as it landed — SQLite accepts
a pragma it will not apply rather than raising, so a
.B journal_mode
that cannot change inside a transaction, a
.B max_page_count
clamped to the pages in use, and an unrecognised value all look like success
otherwise. A pragma cannot be applied over unwritten changes.
.TP
.BR W ", " Ctrl\-s
Write the changes. Every edit is buffered in one savepoint — a cell, a row, an
import, a schema edit, a statement run in the editor — and nothing reaches the
file until this. While anything is unwritten the row grid reads its pages on the
store's own connection, because no other connection can see an open transaction,
and VACUUM, ANALYZE, REINDEX and \-\-backup refuse to run.
.TP
.B R
Revert the changes: roll the savepoint back to the state before the first
unwritten edit. Quitting or going back to the file list with unwritten changes
asks first.
.TP
.B S
Open the schema screen: every object with its CREATE statement, and the
designers that edit them. On that screen
.BR Enter " opens the table or index designer, " a " starts a new table, "
.BR i " a new index on the selected table, " d " drops the object after asking,"
and
.B y
copies its CREATE statement. In a designer the arrows move by row and field,
.B Enter
edits a field,
.B Space
toggles a flag,
.BR a " / " d
add and drop a column,
.BR J " / " K
reorder,
.B W
writes and
.B Esc
cancels. Only renaming a table, renaming a column, appending one and dropping
one are native ALTER TABLE; every other edit runs SQLite's documented rebuild in
one transaction, with foreign keys off and PRAGMA foreign_key_check before the
commit.
.TP
.BR k ", " Up
Previous table (left pane) or previous row (right pane).
.TP
.BR j ", " Down
Next table or next row.
.TP
.BR h ", " l ", " Left ", " Right
Previous / next column (row grid only).
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-f ", " Ctrl\-b ", " PageDown ", " PageUp
Page forward / back.
.TP
.B e
Edit the selected cell.
.TP
.B a
Insert a default row.
.TP
.B d
Delete the selected row (confirms).
.TP
.B :
Run a SQL statement.
.SS rkyv mode
.TP
.BR 0 ", " 1 ", " 2 ", " 3
Records / Info / Strings / Hex. Key
.B 0
is available only when the archive format was recognized.
.TP
.B Enter
In the Records view, open the detail screen for the selected record.
.TP
.BR j ", " k
Scroll the record list, the string list or the hex dump.
.TP
.BR PageDown ", " PageUp
Scroll the hex dump by 16 lines.
.SS Recent-files picker
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " gg ", " G
Move.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the highlighted file.
.TP
.BR / ", " n ", " N
Search file names.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Quit without opening anything.
.SS Detail screen
.TP
.B v
Cycle how the value renders: auto (text when it looks textual, else hex), hex, or
text.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " g ", " G
Scroll the value pane.
.TP
.B y
Copy the value.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Enter
Return to the main view.
.SS Modal input (edit, SQL, search)
.TP
.B Enter
Commit \(em save the cell, run the statement, or accept the pattern and jump to
the first match.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel the modal and return to browsing. Nothing is written.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the last character.
.SH 6. SEARCH
.B /
opens a prompt; Enter stores the pattern and jumps to the first match.
.B n
and
.B N
then walk matches forward and backward. The pattern is a plain substring, not a
regular expression. What is scanned depends on the mode and, in SQLite mode, on
which pane has focus:
.TP
.B SQLite \(em table list
Table names, case-insensitively. Wraps at the ends.
.TP
.B SQLite \(em rows
The whole table, not just the loaded page: a
.B "SELECT rowid ... WHERE <any column> LIKE ?"
walks from the current row in the search direction, wraps from the far edge when
nothing is found ahead, and the view pages to the row that matched. Matching is
case-insensitive, as SQL LIKE is for ASCII.
.TP
.B rkyv \(em Records
Record keys, case-insensitively. Wraps at the ends.
.TP
.B rkyv \(em Strings
The extracted string runs, case-insensitively. Wraps at the ends.
.TP
.B rkyv \(em Hex
The raw bytes, as a case-sensitive byte match; the view scrolls to the line
holding the hit and the status line reports its offset. This search does
.I not
wrap \(em it stops at the end (or the start, going backward).
.TP
.B Picker
The recorded file paths, case-insensitively. Wraps at the ends.
.SH 7. RECENT FILES
Every successful open is appended to the front of the list at
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/zdbview/recent
.RI ( ~/.cache/zdbview/recent
when
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
is unset). The recorded kind is the one actually used, so a
.B .db
file that turned out to be an archive is remembered as
.BR rkyv .
.PP
The file is plain text, one entry per line, tab-separated:
.PP
.RS 4
.I unix-seconds<TAB>kind<TAB>absolute-path
.RE
.PP
Paths are canonicalized, so the same file reached by different relative paths or
symlinks dedupes to one entry, which moves back to the front on re-open. The
list is capped at 50 entries and written to a temporary file that is then
renamed over the original, so a concurrent reader sees either the old list or
the new one, never a half-written one. Malformed lines are skipped on load
rather than aborting, and the picker filters out entries whose file has since
been deleted.
.SH 8. ARCHITECTURE
.TP
.B src/main.rs
CLI parsing, terminal setup and teardown, and the open sequence: resolve the
file (argument or picker) \(rA detect \(rA open \(rA record in the MRU \(rA run.
.TP
.B src/store.rs
.B Kind
detection from the header magic, and the
.B Store
enum that owns whichever backend was selected.
.TP
.B src/sqlite.rs
The SQLite backend: table listing, paged
.BR RowsView s
with rowids, and the update / insert / delete / exec operations. This is the
layer the tests drive.
.TP
.B src/rkyv_inspect.rs
The structural inspector: file load, printable-run extraction, hex-line
formatting.
.TP
.B src/formats.rs
The format registry: byte-compatible copies of the recognized producers'
archive types, magic detection, validated decode to key/value records, and the
fallback to the structural views on any mismatch.
.TP
.B src/mru.rs
The recent-files store: load, record, dedupe, cap, atomic write, relative-age
formatting.
.TP
.B src/export.rs
Dependency-free CSV and JSON writers shared by the interactive
.B x
key and the non-interactive
.B \-\-export
path.
.TP
.B src/clipboard.rs
OSC 52 clipboard copy, including the hand-rolled base64 the escape carries.
.TP
.B src/app.rs
The interactive layer: application state, focus and modal handling, key
dispatch, search, and all ratatui rendering.
.TP
.B tests/backend.rs
Integration tests over the backends without a terminal: a full SQLite CRUD
round-trip, rkyv string extraction and hex formatting, MRU dedupe and ordering,
and detection of a non-SQLite file carrying a
.B .db
name.
.SH 9. EXAMPLES
Browse and edit a SQLite database:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview app.sqlite
.RE
.PP
Inspect an rkyv shard stored under a
.B .db
name \(em detection routes it to the inspector automatically:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview ~/.cache/zshrs/scripts.db
.RE
.PP
Look at the raw page structure of a real SQLite file:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview \-\-rkyv app.sqlite
.RE
.PP
Reopen something from earlier:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview
.RE
.SH 10. LIMITATIONS
.TP
.B rkyv archives are read-only
Recognized formats decode to key/value, but nothing is written back:
re-serializing a shard means reproducing the producer's exact layout, and a
wrong write corrupts a cache the producer then mmaps. Unrecognized archives stay
structural, since guessing field boundaries from an untyped image would produce
plausible, wrong output.
.TP
.B Search is a plain substring
No regular expressions, and no full-text index. SQLite row search runs as a SQL
.B LIKE
over every column of the table, so it is whole-table but linear; a
.B WHERE
clause through
.B :
remains the right tool for a complex predicate.
.TP
.B WITHOUT ROWID tables are read-only
No
.B rowid
means no generic row handle for
.B UPDATE
and
.BR DELETE .
.TP
.B Blobs are readable, not editable
The grid summarizes a blob as
.RI "\(oq" "<blob N bytes>" "\(cq"
and the detail screen shows its bytes as hex or text, but writing binary values
still goes through
.BR : .
.TP
.B Single connection, no transaction UI
Each statement commits on its own. There is no explicit begin/rollback surface.
.SH 11. EXPORT, DETAIL AND CLIPBOARD
.SS Export
.B x
writes the current table as CSV (SQLite) or all decoded records as JSON (rkyv)
to a file in the working directory, named after the table or the archive. The
same rendering is available without the TUI:
.PP
.RS 4
.B zdbview data.db \-\-export json
.br
.B zdbview data.db \-\-export csv
.br
.B zdbview cache.rkyv \-\-export json
.RE
.PP
For SQLite,
.B json
emits an object of
.RI { table :
[rows]} covering every table, while
.B csv
emits the first table alone \(em CSV has no way to express several tables in one
stream. For a recognized rkyv archive both emit the decoded records, each value
blob as a lowercase hex string; an unrecognized archive is an error, because
there is nothing structured to export. The writers are hand-rolled in
.IR src/export.rs ,
so export adds no dependency, and their quoting and escaping are unit-tested.
.SS Detail screen
.B Enter
on a row or record opens a full-screen detail: every field untruncated at the
top, and a scrollable value pane below.
.B v
cycles the value render mode \(em
.B auto
(text when the bytes look textual, else hex),
.B hex
.RB ( xxd
style), or
.B text
(UTF-8). This is how a cell or blob too wide for the grid gets read.
.SS Clipboard
.B y
copies through the
.B OSC 52
terminal escape, written straight to
.IR /dev/tty .
No clipboard library, no X, Wayland or
.BR pbcopy (1)
dependency, and it works over SSH, because the terminal emulator on the near end
is what sets the clipboard. It is best-effort: with no controlling terminal the
copy silently does nothing.
